edulePrint","subType":"yesNoReference","type":"REFERENCE","values":["Y"]},{"name":"gradingScale","subType":"gradingScaleRef","type":"REFERENCE","values":["GRD"]},{"name":"dutchNameShort","type":"STRING","values":["Language Typology (10 EC) SCH"]}],"year":{"code":"2026-2027","id":"2261","names":[{"language":"EN","value":"2026-2027"},{"language":"NL","value":"2026-2027"}],"uid":"07254806-916f-4cca-82a0-2e69a4a95e9f"}},"required":true,"values":[{"name":"evaluate","subType":"yesNoReference","type":"REFERENCE","values":["Y"]}],"weighting":1.00}],"capacity":{"optimum":35.00},"code":"5512VEBE8Y","credits":{"optimum":10.00},"descriptions":[{"language":"EN","text":"Linguistic structures are generally not randomly distributed around the globe: some seem to be universally preferred, others are common in certain parts of the world, or in particular language families. The goal of modern typology is to detect distributional patterns, and to explain them in terms of factors that influence the historical development of languages. Typological work crucially depends on language description, but at the same time, typology feeds back into language description.\n\nDuring this course, we highlight this co-dependency between descriptive linguistics and language typology by studying phonological, morphological, syntactic, and lexical patterns both in individual languages, and across languages. We will study a number of structural similarities and differences between languages, focusing on questions such as: “Which features coincide?” “Why are some patterns much more common than others?” “Are some patterns particularly common in e.g. Africa or South America?” “How can language X be compared to language Y?”\n\nIn doing so, you will become acquainted with the fundamentals of language description and typology. In addition, we will discuss how typological knowledge informs linguistic fieldwork on an undescribed language.","type":{"code":"CO","id":"CO","names":[{"language":"EN","value":"Description"},{"language":"NL","value":"Beschrijving"}],"uid":"9b0c3e97-ad09-42d7-a5bb-afdaf6f1877f"}},{"language":"EN","text":"To be announced","type":{"code":"LI","id":"LI","names":[{"language":"EN","value":"Reading list"},{"language":"NL","value":"Literatuurlijst"}],"uid":"fbd93a9e-ca99-4bcc-807f-39235bb6000b"}},{"language":"EN","text":"### Assessment\n\n* Written examination with short open questions\n* Take home assignment\n\n### Weighing\n\n* Midterm exam 50%\n* Group assignment 50%","type":{"code":"TP","id":"TP","names":[{"language":"EN","value":"Assessment method"},{"language":"NL","value":"Toetsing en weging"}],"uid":"687f9ba4-4330-4ab9-8536-be9ebd2c58c5"}},{"language":"EN","text":"Enrolment through MyStudyMap (button in upper right corner) is mandatory. General information about course and exam enrolment is available on the website ( https://www.student.universiteitleiden.nl/en/administrative-matters/registration--de-registration/course-and-exam-enrolment/course-and-exam-enrolment/).","type":{"code":"ENR","id":"ENR","names":[{"language":"EN","value":"Registration"},{"language":"NL","value":"Inschrijven"}],"uid":"a77a2043-b0a9-450d-b780-1e90a4d3290c"}},{"language":"EN","text":"At the end of this course, you will have:\n\n* an understanding of the ways in which languages are similar (linguistic universals) and different (linguistic diversity), and how you can describe this for individual languages with which you are unacquainted;\n* knowledge of fundamental concepts and methods regarding language typology and language description;\n* basic skills needed to apply typological principles on primary data (gathered in fieldwork for example);\n* basic skills in translating structures in individual languages into a typological framework.","type":{"code":"LO","id":"LO","names":[{"language":"EN","value":"Course objectives"},{"language":"NL","value":"Leerdoelen"}],"uid":"b54eb210-96e8-490d-aadb-a70571a665d9"}},{"language":"EN","text":"Introduction linguistics (e.g. Languages of the World or equivalent)","type":{"code":"AR","id":"AR","names":[{"language":"EN","value":"Admission requirements"},{"language":"NL","value":"Toegangseisen"}],"uid":"2c481598-5834-4422-a338-1eb346d6f52c"}},{"language":"EN","text":"For substantive questions, contact the lecturer(s) (listed in the right information bar).  For questions about enrolment, admission, etc., contact the Education Administration Office: osz-oa-reuvensplaats@hum.leidenuniv.nl","type":{"code":"CG","id":"CG","names":[{"language":"EN","value":"Contact"},{"language":"NL","value":"Contact"}],"uid":"35f7e88a-830a-4877-b467-86a700954d19"}},{"language":"EN","text":"Lecture and seminar","type":{"code":"WV","id":"WV","names":[{"language":"EN","value":"Teaching method"},{"language":"NL","value":"Onderwijsvorm"}],"uid":"26f0e596-e48d-46ff-8c8b-9b9688699943"}},{"language":"EN","text":"The timetables are available through My Timetable (see the button in the upper right corner).","type":{"code":"SC","id":"SC","names":[{"language":"EN","value":"Schedule"},{"language":"NL","value":"Rooster"}],"uid":"939373d2-217d-4dde-9c37-6f6101f1ab75"}},{"language":"EN","text":"### Resit\n\nStudents can resit the midterm. If they fail the take-home assignment, they can take an extra assignment.\n\n### Review and feedback\n\nHow and when an exam review will take place will be disclosed together with the publication of the exam results at the latest. If a student requests a review within 30 days after publication of the exam results, an exam review will have to be organized.","type":{"code":"RRD","id":"RRD","names":[{"language":"EN","value":"Resit, review & feedback"},{"language":"NL","value":"Herkansing, inzage & nabespreking"}],"uid":"ef7be178-91d7-4f56-a40b-891976f3af1c"}}],"faculty":{"code":"FGW","id":"FGW","names":[{"language":"EN","value":"Humanities"},{"language":"NL","value":"Geesteswetenschappen"}],"uid":"28c8aabb-f896-444f-895c-63ef88ba9307"},"id":"255466","key":104878,"methods":[{"method":{"category":"T","code":